C2 phase 2 — CL-2 ingester WAL replication: zero acknowledged loss on a node death
2026-08-10 · ingester-a + ingester-b (bench/compose/timelakedb-cluster.yml)
================================================================================

THE PROPERTY (P1-1). A single instance is a single point of failure. Two
ingesters pair up: each write is replicated to the peer's durable replica
WAL BEFORE the 204, so an acknowledged write is durable on two nodes and
survives either one dying. This is the phase that makes "survives node
loss" true.

Drilled on a real ingester pair (each the other's TIMELAKE_PEERS peer;
writes go to A directly — the router that will front them is C2 phase 3):

  12/12 checks passed

LEG 1 — DEGRADED MODE (peer down must not fail writes; PR-7)
  Stop ingester-b, keep writing to A:
    - 4000 lines still acked (204) on LOCAL durability — writes did not fail
    - timelake_cl2_degraded == 1 (gauge)
    - timelake_cl2_degraded_events_total == 1 (entered degraded once)
    - the named alarm CL2_REPLICATION_DEGRADED logged exactly once (not per
      write — a flapping peer stays legible)
  Start ingester-b again, write once more:
    - timelake_cl2_degraded back to 0 — the node left degraded on its own

  The honest cost, stated in the alarm: while degraded, a second failure
  can lose the un-replicated writes. Availability over the second replica
  when the pair is already half up — the deliberate trade, made loud.

LEG 2 — ZERO ACKNOWLEDGED LOSS on a node death
  Both healthy. Write 20,000 lines to A:
    - A has all 20,000
    - A replicated to B (timelake_cl2_replicated_total = 5 frames = 5 batches)
    - A is NOT degraded
    - B received the frames into its replica WAL (replica_frames_total = 5)
    - B has NOT applied them — the replica frames are DORMANT, so B does not
      double-flush its peer's live rows (a query on B sees 0)
  SIGKILL ingester-a. Recover on B (POST /internal/v1/recover):
    - {"recovered":5}
    - B now serves all 20,000 lines A had acknowledged — ZERO LOSS, exact
    - timelake_cl2_recovered_total reflects the replay

WHAT MAKES IT WORK
  - Replicate-before-ack: the write path appends the local WAL, ships the
    frame to the peer's internal listener, and only then applies + 204s.
    A down peer degrades (availability), never fails the write.
  - The peer holds frames in a DURABLE replica WAL (its own fsync'd log),
    dormant until recovery — so no double-flush in steady state, and the
    copy survives the recovering node's own restart (unit-tested).
  - Recovery replays the replica WAL into the engine and flushes. Overlap
    with rows the dead peer already flushed to S3 is safe: LWW dedup (FR-5)
    collapses it at compaction. Recovery is idempotent.

role=all IS UNCHANGED. A lone node has no peer, so no replicator, no
replica WAL, no internal listener, and no CL-2 metrics — the write path is
byte-for-byte today's. Pinned by the unit test
`a_lone_node_has_no_replicator_and_writes_normally` and the full suite.

SCOPE / NOT YET
  - Recovery is an EXPLICIT operation here (the drill, or an operator / the
    router on a confirmed peer death). Automatic health-triggered failover
    is a later cluster phase (needs the router + health).
  - The router (hash → ingester pair) is C2 phase 3; this drill writes to an
    ingester directly.
  - Intra-cluster transport is plaintext HTTP on TIMELAKE_CLUSTER_ADDR;
    required-mTLS is C3 (the verifier is already shipped).
  - Cost: replication adds one peer round-trip per batch to the write path
    (ARCHITECTURE risk 9) — expected, and the reason degraded mode exists.

WORKSPACE: 135 tests (+4 in-process replication), clippy clean, fmt clean.
